HST Office: HST Sheets has the worksheet functions of the leading spreadsheet application that the engine lacked,
and CHAR, CODE, CELL("filename") and INFO give that application's results (owner request 2026-11-14).

New functions (UI, .xlsx and ODF names; Function Wizard text in English and Vietnamese):
- ARRAYTOTEXT, VALUETOTEXT: a range/array or a value as text, format 0 concise or 1 strict (quoted text).
  ARRAYTOTEXT is the upstream implementation (LibreOffice master 21b9909af + a8e87aba0, MPL-2.0). An error
  argument becomes its text (both are error-evaluating functions).
- DBCS: half-width to full-width letters and katakana, the JIS conversion under its own name.
- PHONETIC: no phonetic guide is stored, so it returns the text of the referenced cell (upper-left cell of a
  range), as the reference application does for text without furigana; a number gives "".
- PERCENTOF(subset; all): SUM(subset)/SUM(all), text ignored, errors propagate, #DIV/0! for a zero total.
- REGEXTEST, REGEXEXTRACT (mode 0 first match, 1 all matches as a row, 2 groups of the first match as a row;
  #N/A when nothing matches), REGEXREPLACE (occurrence 0 all, n-th, -n from the end), on the ICU engine of
  REGEX(); case_sensitivity 0/1; an invalid pattern or option is #VALUE!.
- TRIMRANGE(range; rows; cols): the range without its blank edge rows/columns (0 none, 1 leading, 2 trailing,
  3 both, default 3); returns a reference for a reference, an array for an array, #REF! when all is blank.
- BINOM.DIST.RANGE is the UI and .xlsx name of the existing B function (same arguments and results). Upstream
  wrote it to .xlsx as B(), which the reference application does not know; B stays accepted as input and in
  older .xlsx files, ODF keeps BINOM.DIST.RANGE.
Files: .xlsx _xlfn.<NAME> (PHONETIC has no prefix, it is a BIFF8 function), ODF COM.MICROSOFT.<NAME>, the
binary .xls tables too. New code: sc/source/core/tool/interpr_excelcompat.cxx and interpr_excelregex.cxx.

Semantics:
- CHAR/CODE use the system ANSI code page like the reference application: on Windows the thread encoding
  (GetACP: 1258 Vietnamese, 1252 English, as before); on Linux/macOS, which have no ANSI code page and gave the
  first UTF-8 byte (CODE("Đ") = 196), the Windows code page of the locale setting (vi-VN 1258, en-US 1252).
  OpenFormula leaves the code page to the implementation, so ODF documents need no special case.
- CELL("filename"): C:\dir\[file.xlsx]Sheet with the native path of the OS (/home/u/[file.xlsx]Sheet on Linux)
  and "" for a document never saved. OpenDocument files keep 'file:///dir/file.ods'#$Sheet, which existing ODF
  formulas parse (MID/FIND on "#$"), unless the string reference syntax is explicitly an Excel one.
- INFO("system") is "pcdos" ("mac" on macOS) instead of LINUX/WNT/MACOSX; INFO("osversion") is
  "Windows (64-bit) NT 10.00" on Windows 10/11 (was "Windows (32-bit) NT 5.01"), "Macintosh (Intel|ARM)
  Version x.y" on macOS; Linux keeps its real version, the reference application has no Linux value.
- Functions this product does not implement because they need the reference vendor's online services or
  Windows-only components (STOCKHISTORY, TRANSLATE, DETECTLANGUAGE, COPILOT, PY, FIELDVALUE, IMAGE, CUBE*, RTD,
  CALL, REGISTER.ID): an .xlsx keeps their formulas and cached results on open and writes them back unchanged.
  The compiler used to lower-case such unknown names (_xlfn.stockhistory); in OOXML formulas it now keeps the
  file's spelling. A recalculation still gives #NAME?.

License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0).
